# /etc/decdn/decdn.env — the deCDN node's secret environment file.
#
# Provision this ON THE TARGET HOST, not on the control machine: systemd reads it
# as root (EnvironmentFile=) before dropping to the `decdn` user, and the decdn_node
# Ansible role deliberately never reads its contents back. Leave decdn_rpc_url empty
# in inventory and the role leaves this file alone, enforcing only 0600 ownership —
# so the RPC key (and any cloud credentials below) never sit on a laptop.
#
# This file is a TEMPLATE that lives on the control machine; copy its contents to
# the host by hand (or paste the two commands below). Create it as ROOT: on a host
# that has never been converged the `decdn` account does not exist yet — the role
# creates it, and chowns this file to decdn:decdn on the converge that follows.
#
#   umask 077                                  # never briefly world-readable
#   sudo mkdir -p /etc/decdn
#   sudo tee /etc/decdn/decdn.env >/dev/null   # then paste the lines, Ctrl-D
#   sudo chmod 600 /etc/decdn/decdn.env        # sudo may apply its own umask
#
# After editing it out of band, re-run `make deploy` — the role notices the change
# and restarts the unit — or restart by hand: `sudo systemctl restart decdn-node`.
#
# FORMAT: systemd's EnvironmentFile parser is shell-LIKE but is not a shell — no
# expansion, no `export`, one KEY=value per line. It SKIPS a line it cannot parse
# (logging a warning to `journalctl -u decdn-node` that is easy to miss), so the
# variable is simply absent at runtime — surfacing much later as an auth failure.
# Single-quote any value containing a space, quote, backslash, `$` or backtick.
# The role's `decdn config validate` step sources this file with bash, which —
# unlike systemd — WOULD expand `$VAR` and execute `$(...)`; quoting avoids both.

# REQUIRED. The chain RPC endpoint — may embed a provider API key, which is why
# this file exists at all. Public endpoints work for light use; run your own or use
# a provider for production reliability.
DECDN_RPC_URL=https://your-endpoint.example/rpc

# OPTIONAL. Static AWS credentials for an S3 cache pull-through origin. The role
# never writes these into node.toml (that file is 0640 and diffable) — it only
# templates `source = "default-chain"` there (decdn_cache_origin_s3_use_default_chain:
# true), and the AWS default credential chain reads exactly these variables.
# Unnecessary on an instance with an IAM role / instance profile.
#AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIA...
#AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
#AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=...            # assume-role / SSO flows only
